From: Andrey Korshunov <razor1702@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] nl: report netlink errors back to the caller

bridge_nl_error_cb() is installed as the NL_CB_CUSTOM error handler, and
it ends every path with NL_SKIP. libnl then treats the message as
consumed and nl_wait_for_ack() returns 0, so every rejected request looks
like a success to the caller. NL_STOP makes libnl return
-nl_syserr2nlerr(err->error) instead, which is what the default handler
does.

bridger_nl_flow_offload_add() depends on this: it walks the device's
offload chain and stops at the first device that accepts the flower
filter. On a GL.iNet GL-BE14000 (MediaTek MT7988 with a Motorcomm YT9224
switch) the DSA user port rejects the skip_sw filter with -EOPNOTSUPP -
the switch only offloads ACL rules, not a redirect to a Wi-Fi netdev -
but the error was swallowed, so bridger recorded the flow as offloaded
and never tried the conduit, where the MediaTek PPE does accept it and
forwards the flow in hardware.

The error carries no ext-ack message here, so it took the
NLM_F_ACK_TLVS path out; both returns are changed for consistency.

Signed-off-by: Andrey Korshunov <razor1702@gmail.com>
--- a/nl.c
+++ b/nl.c
@@ -901,7 +901,7 @@
 		return NL_SKIP;
 
 	if (!(nlh->nlmsg_flags & NLM_F_ACK_TLVS))
-		return NL_SKIP;
+		return NL_STOP;
 
 	if (!(nlh->nlmsg_flags & NLM_F_CAPPED))
 		ack_len += err->msg.nlmsg_len - sizeof(*nlh);
@@ -915,7 +915,7 @@
 
 	D("Netlink error(%d): %s\n", err->error, errstr);
 
-	return NL_SKIP;
+	return NL_STOP;
 }
 
 struct nl_sock *
